NNAAMMEE
     ttiimmee — time command execution

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ttiimmee [--llpp] _u_t_i_l_i_t_y [_a_r_g_u_m_e_n_t _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ttiimmee executes and times _u_t_i_l_i_t_y.  After the _u_t_i_l_i_t_y finishes, ttiimmee writes
     the total time elapsed, the time consumed by system overhead, and the
     time used to execute _u_t_i_l_i_t_y to the standard error stream.  Times are re‐
     ported in seconds.

     The options are as follows:

     --ll      The contents of the _r_u_s_a_g_e structure are printed.

     --pp      The output is formatted as specified by IEEE Std 1003.2-1992
             (“POSIX.2”).

FFIILLEESS
     /usr/include/sys/resource.h

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The ttiimmee utility exits with one of the following values:

     1-125   An error occurred in the ttiimmee utility.
     126     The _u_t_i_l_i_t_y was found but could not be invoked.
     127     The _u_t_i_l_i_t_y could not be found.

     Otherwise, the exit status of ttiimmee shall be that of _u_t_i_l_i_t_y.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     csh(1), ksh(1), getrusage(2)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     The ttiimmee utility is compliant with the IEEE Std 1003.1-2008 (“POSIX.1”)
     specification.

     The flag [--ll] is an extension to that specification.

     ttiimmee also exists as a built-in to csh(1) and ksh(1), though with a dif‐
     ferent syntax.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     A ttiimmee command appeared in Version 3 AT&T UNIX.

